; 31-arity.kurt

; `arity SYMBOL N` declares that `SYMBOL` always takes exactly `N`
; arguments, written by juxtaposition (space, `minimal.kurt`'s built-in
; infix operator) -- this is how ordinary function/predicate symbols like
; `f x y` are declared, as opposed to infix/prefix/postfix operators.

arity f 2

var a, b
parse f a b              ; (f a b)

; the shape of the arguments, as the symbol is used: `_` for an argument, `(_, _)`
; for a pair -- `g(a, b)` is `g` applied to one argument, the pair `(a, b)`, and
; Kurt checks each use (`g(a, b, c)` is an error, and so is `g(c)` for a constant `c`;
; `g(a)` with the variable `a` is fine: a variable may stand for a pair)
arity g (_, _)
parse g(a, b)            ; (g (() (, a b))): `()` is the tuple, `,` the list in it

; the shape can mix both: a finite sum `mysum i (1, n) T` takes the variable,
; the range as a pair, and the body -- three arguments, the second a pair. This is
; how analysis.kurt declares its `sum` (`arity sum _ (_, _) _`, also a `binder`,
; 32-binder.kurt), and integral.kurt its `integral`.
load number
var n
arity mysum _ (_, _) _
parse mysum i (1, n) (i * i)          ; (mysum i (() (, 1 n)) (* i i))
expect "EvalError" "got `mysum i 1 (i * i)`"
    parse mysum i 1 (i * i)           ; the range must be a pair

; without `arity`, a symbol is curried and nothing is checked: `h a b` is
; `(h a) b`, stored as one call
parse h a b              ; (h a b)

; Compare `arity` with `prefix` (28-prefix.kurt; doc/dev-notes.md has the history): `arity`
; fixes both how many arguments a symbol takes *and* the binding power used
; to grab them (space's), while `prefix` lets you pick a custom binding
; power for a single argument instead.
